<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Forest Bathing and Other Biohacks – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Managing Stress in Changing Times</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Forest Bathing and Other Biohacks</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write reviews on general products or services. Interactive: Revise classmates' essays on general topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a comparative review of two wellness strategies, integrating real-life examples from local surroundings or meditation apps (e.g., "The daily meditation app I downloaded is great, but hiking in the rainforest provides a unique sensory experience.")<br>Can edit classmates' essays on wellness practices, suggesting improvements and clarifying points (e.g., "Consider explaining how breathing exercises reduce stress.")<br>Can revise essays by adding suggestions of stress management techniques (e.g., "You should include how meditation helps you focus.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 10%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by asking students to think about their personal top two stress-management techniques. Students then write down three adjectives to describe each technique and briefly explain why they chose them. For example: "For me, <b>listening</b> to <b>soothing</b> music is very <b>relaxing</b> and <b>calming</b>. <b>Practicing</b> deep <b>breathing</b> techniques is also very <b>beneficial</b> for my <b>focus</b> and overall <b>wellness</b>." This activates prior knowledge and vocabulary from previous lessons. Students share their ideas in pairs orally, but write down notes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write a simple comparative statement about two wellness strategies, highlighting the use of gerunds and modals for advice. Example: "While <b>meditating</b> in a <b>quiet spot</b> is effective for me, <b>going for a walk</b> in <b>nature</b> (like the rainforest) provides a unique sensory experience. You <b>should try</b> both to see what works best for your <b>mind</b> and <b>body</b>." The teacher also models the pronunciation of /u:/ in "soothing" and "smooth" and vowel reduction in "mindfulness."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What type of words did I use to describe the actions, like 'meditating' or 'going'?" (Expected answer: Verbs ending in -ing, gerunds.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I want to give someone advice, which type of word can I use, like 'should' or 'could'?" (Expected answer: Modals.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Managing Stress in Changing Times".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short blog post excerpt (200-250 words) from a "wellness strategies review" website comparing two popular stress management techniques, such as "Forest Bathing" and "Mindfulness Meditation Apps." The text should integrate the target vocabulary (e.g., "biohack," "relaxation," "calming," "reduce stress," "improve focus") and grammatical structures (gerunds: "practicing mindfulness," "spending time in nature"; modals: "you should consider," "it could help"; present continuous for trends: "people are increasingly embracing," "these practices are gaining").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying the main ideas and supporting details in written texts about wellness strategies, recognizing the author's purpose in providing a comparative review, and understanding how advice is given in written form.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What are the two main wellness strategies being compared in the text?" "What is the overall recommendation the author gives?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match phrases describing benefits (e.g., "reduces stress," "improves focus") to the correct wellness strategy mentioned in the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identifying Grammar:</b> Students underline all gerunds and modal verbs for advice they find in the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on accurate written production of sentences using gerunds (e.g., "Practicing mindfulness," "Listening to music"), modals for advice (e.g., "You should try," "You could consider"), and present continuous for trends (e.g., "People are practicing mindfulness"). Key vocabulary: "breathing," "relaxation," "forest bathing," "mindfulness," "meditation," "stress," "wellness," "focus," "techniques," "practices," "beneficial," "calming," "effective," "stress-free."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students complete sentences using a gerund form of a given verb and one of the target vocabulary words. Example: "__________ (relax) in a __________ (quiet spot) is a great way to __________ (reduce) __________ (stress)." (Answer: "Relaxing in a quiet spot is a great way to reduce stress.") They also complete sentences using modals for advice. Example: "If you're feeling overwhelmed, you __________ (modal) __________ (try) some deep __________ (breathing) __________ (techniques)." (Answer: "If you're feeling overwhelmed, you should try some deep breathing techniques.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Combination & Expansion):</b> Students combine two simple sentences into one more complex sentence using gerunds or modals, focusing on comparison or advice. For example: "I listen to music. It helps me relax." becomes "<b>Listening</b> to music helps me <b>relax</b>." "Meditation is good. You can reduce stress." becomes "You <b>should try</b> meditation for <b>reducing stress</b>." They also expand on trend sentences using the present continuous and additional details: "People are discovering biohacks." -> "People <b>are discovering</b> new <b>biohacks</b> for <b>improving</b> their <b>wellness</b> and <b>managing stress</b> effectively."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Sentence Check):</b> Students exchange their completed sentences and correct each other's work for grammatical accuracy (gerunds, modals, present continuous) and appropriate vocabulary use. They use a checklist focusing on these elements. For example, "Is the gerund used correctly?" "Is the advice given politely using a modal?" "Are the vocabulary words relevant to wellness?" They practice alternating full and reduced vowels when reading words like "mindfulness" or "beneficial" as they check.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Managing Stress in Changing Times".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Written Product – Students will write a comparative review (200-250 words) of two wellness strategies. They must choose one strategy from the "Forest Bathing and Other Biohacks" theme (e.g., forest bathing, deep breathing, sound baths) and compare it with another stress management technique (e.g., meditation app, Zumba classes, listening to music). They should integrate real-life examples from local surroundings (e.g., "hiking in Metropolitan Park," "a park bench at Parque Omar") or personal experiences (e.g., "the daily meditation app I downloaded").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students independently select two wellness strategies they are familiar with or have researched.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They plan their review by brainstorming pros and cons for each strategy and how they affect the <b>mind</b> and <b>body</b>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students write their comparative review, ensuring they use:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gerunds (e.g., "<b>Practicing</b> mindfulness is <b>beneficial</b>," "<b>Spending</b> time in <b>nature</b> is very <b>calming</b>").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Modals for advice (e.g., "You <b>should try</b> both <b>techniques</b>," "You <b>could consider</b> <b>attending</b> a wellness <b>retreat</b>").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Present continuous for trends (e.g., "People <b>are practicing</b> more <b>biohacks</b>," "More and more individuals <b>are embracing</b> <b>forest bathing</b>").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A variety of the target vocabulary (nouns: "breathing," "relaxation," "stress," "wellness," "focus," "habits"; verbs: "practice," "relax," "meditate," "calm," "reduce," "improve"; adjectives: "relaxing," "mindful," "deep," "soothing," "beneficial," "stress-free").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They include specific examples to support their points (e.g., "When I attend Zumba classes at the community center, I feel more energized and focused afterward.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency and coherence in written expression, accurate application of target grammar and vocabulary, and effective comparison and advice-giving within the review format.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer Assessment using a checklist and rubric for written comparative reviews.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their comparative reviews with a partner. Each student reads their partner's essay and provides written feedback using a guided peer-editing checklist. The checklist focuses on the specific learning outcomes:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Does the review clearly compare two wellness strategies?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are gerunds, modals for advice, and present continuous for trends used correctly and naturally?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Is the target vocabulary (e.g., "mindfulness," "relaxation," "stress-free," "beneficial") used effectively?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are there real-life examples or personal experiences integrated? (e.g., "The daily meditation app I downloaded is great...")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Does the essay offer clear suggestions or advice on stress management? (e.g., "You should include how meditation helps you focus.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are there suggestions for improvements or clarifying points? (e.g., "Consider explaining how breathing exercises reduce stress.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Partners should offer polite and constructive feedback (Sociolinguistic: "You could try relaxation techniques like deep breathing.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the comparative review.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate and appropriate use of target grammar (gerunds, modals, present continuous).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Variety and accuracy of target vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of real-life examples and personal experiences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effectiveness of peer feedback and suggestions for improvement.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of writing your comparative review and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one specific thing you learned from reviewing your classmate's essay?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now in writing a comparative review using gerunds, modals, and present continuous for trends?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific aspect of your writing or your peer's writing would you like to improve on for future tasks related to stress management?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., clear instructions for the review, effective peer feedback, good engagement with real-life examples).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson or future writing tasks? (e.g., more direct instruction on paragraph structuring for comparative reviews, additional sentence starters for advice, a quick review of common writing errors found in peer assessment).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the students meet the B1.2 writing complexity? Were the comparisons sufficiently detailed?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize their comparative reviews orally or in writing, preparing a short presentation to share their recommended wellness strategies and the rationale behind their choices, integrating their writing with speaking skills. They might also mediate information for a wider audience.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>